In framework-engine, change AbstractReporter from:
abstract class AbstractReporter extends Reporter
to:
abstract class AbstractReporter implements \Lucinda\STDERR\ErrorReporter
Its entry point becomes:
public function report(
    \Throwable $error,
    ?\Throwable $previous = null
): void
It should no longer depend on:
- Lucinda\STDERR\Request
- reporter XML;
- a resolved STDERR route;
- run().
There is one behavioral decision to make: the old implementation chose logger severity using the route’s ErrorType. That information is no longer 
available when report() is first called—the front controller deliberately reports before parsing error configuration at 
[FrontController.php (line 108)](//wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/aherne/framework/framework-skeleton/vendor/lucinda/errors-mvc/src/FrontController.php:108).
I recommend that the Engine reporter:
- default to Logger::error($error);
- expose a protected hook for applications wanting severity classification;
- treat $previous as contextual information for failures occurring inside the error-handling process;
- avoid depending on rendering-route classification.
Your new skeleton class at [src/STDERR/ErrorReporter.php (line 7)](//wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/aherne/framework/framework-skeleton/src/STDERR/ErrorReporter.php:7) 
already implements the correct interface shape. Once Engine provides the proper bridge, it could either extend that Engine abstraction or be 
replaced with a configured concrete Engine reporter.
The reference search confirms that the obsolete Reporter is no longer used by errors-mvc itself; only the stale Engine AbstractReporter still imports it.
So the safe order is:
1. Rewrite Engine AbstractReporter.
2. Add reporter integration tests.
3. Delete errors-mvc/src/Reporter.php.
4. Add : void to ErrorReporter::report().
5. Update documentation and release both packages together.
